{"choices":[{"label":"0","text":"It is a premise to support the conclusion that "all words can cause people to respond positively or negatively. "."},{"label":"1","text":"It is a conclusion, and the only evidence that supports it is the claim that people ’s reactions to words are only affected by the meaning and pronunciation of words."},{"label":"2","text":"It is a conclusion that is partially supported by meaningful words that can elicit positive or negative reactions."},{"label":"3","text":"It is a premise to support the conclusion that "people's reactions to words are affected not only by the meaning of the words, but also by the pronunciation of the words"."}],"context":"Words such as "kindness" and "awesome" can cause people to respond positively; while words such as "evil" and "nausea" can cause people to react negatively. Recent psychology experiments have shown that many meaningless words can also cause people to respond positively or negatively. This shows that people's reactions to words are not only affected by the meaning of words, but also by the pronunciation of words.","poetic_rationale":"Meaningful words provoke reactions through their sense,
